 Let's denote the first term of the arithmetic series as $a$ and the common difference as $d$.  
The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic series is given by
\begin{equation*}
S_n=\frac{n}{2}(2a+(n-1)d)
\end{equation*}
Therefore, for the first 5 terms:
\begin{equation*}
S_5=\frac{5}{2}(2a+(5-1)d)=70
\end{equation*}
And for the first 10 terms:
\begin{equation*}
S_{10}=\frac{10}{2}(2a+(10-1)d)=210
\end{equation*}
Simplifying these equations:
\begin{equation*}
10a+20d=140 \hspace{1cm} (1)
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
20a+90d=420 \hspace{1cm} (2)
\end{equation*}
To solve this system of linear equations, we can multiply equation (1) by 2 and subtract it from equation (2):
\begin{align*}
40a+60d &= 480 \\
-(20a+90d) &=-420 \\
\hline
20a-30d &= 60 \\
6a-9d &= 18
\end{align*}
Now we solve for $d$: $6a-9d=18$ which yields $9d=6a-18$.
Now we substitute any of the variables $a, d$ into either one of the two equations (1) or (2) to find the value of the other one.  Let's choose (1) and substitute $9d = 6a-18$:
\begin{align*}
10a+20(6a-18)/9 &= 140 \\
90a+120a-360 &= 1260 \\
210a &= 1620 \\
a &= 8
\end{align*}
To find $d$, we can plug $a=8$ into one of the equations above.  Let's plug it into the equation $9d=6a-18$:
